1
00:00:00,427 --> 00:00:04,290
Today, tarot is famous for reading the future.

2
00:00:04,290 --> 00:00:07,725
But it began as something else entirely:

3
00:00:07,725 --> 00:00:12,103
a luxurious card game, made for Italian nobility.

4
00:00:13,270 --> 00:00:19,690
Around 1370, playing cards arrived in Europe, carried from the Islamic world,

5
00:00:19,690 --> 00:00:22,559
from the Mamluks of Egypt.

6
00:00:23,484 --> 00:00:31,416
They had four suits: cups, coins, swords, and polo sticks, which in Italy became batons.

7
00:00:32,446 --> 00:00:38,390
In the 15th century, Milan was ruled by Duke Filippo Maria Visconti.

8
00:00:38,390 --> 00:00:42,210
Italian courts adored games, and art.

9
00:00:43,139 --> 00:00:46,601
Around 1440, someone had an idea:

10
00:00:46,601 --> 00:00:52,767
add a series of illustrated cards that could beat all the others.

11
00:00:52,767 --> 00:00:56,107
Trumps. In Italian: trionfi.

12
00:00:57,081 --> 00:01:04,195
In 1441, Bianca Maria Visconti, the duke's daughter, married Francesco Sforza.

13
00:01:04,195 --> 00:01:07,472
The two families became one.

14
00:01:08,510 --> 00:01:12,757
For them, the Visconti-Sforza decks were created:

15
00:01:12,757 --> 00:01:16,110
painted by hand, with real gold leaf.

16
00:01:16,110 --> 00:01:21,030
The work is attributed to the painter Bonifacio Bembo.

17
00:01:21,964 --> 00:01:26,567
Of the most famous deck, 74 of its 78 cards survive.

18
00:01:26,567 --> 00:01:32,780
Today they're divided between New York, Bergamo and a private collection.

19
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:35,388
Two have never been found:

20
00:01:35,388 --> 00:01:38,219
the Devil, and the Tower.

21
00:01:39,395 --> 00:01:43,382
For more than 300 years, tarot was simply that:

22
00:01:43,382 --> 00:01:49,772
a game. Tarocchi. A trick-taking game, where trumps defeat every other card.

23
00:01:50,703 --> 00:01:54,201
The game spread through France and Switzerland.

24
00:01:54,201 --> 00:02:00,542
Printed from carved woodblocks, it took on the look we now call the Tarot de Marseille.

25
00:02:01,480 --> 00:02:07,541
In 1781, the French scholar Antoine Court de Gébelin declared that tarot

26
00:02:07,541 --> 00:02:10,950
was a secret book from ancient Egypt.

27
00:02:10,950 --> 00:02:15,569
There was no evidence at all. But the idea stuck.

28
00:02:16,704 --> 00:02:22,281
Soon after, a fortune teller called Etteilla created the first tarot deck

29
00:02:22,281 --> 00:02:24,484
designed for divination.

30
00:02:24,484 --> 00:02:27,400
And the game became an oracle.

31
00:02:28,365 --> 00:02:35,831
In the 19th century, Éliphas Lévi linked the 22 major arcana to the 22 letters

32
00:02:35,831 --> 00:02:38,522
of the Hebrew alphabet.

33
00:02:38,522 --> 00:02:44,552
And the Golden Dawn turned tarot into a system of symbols.

34
00:02:45,434 --> 00:02:51,817
In 1909, the artist Pamela Colman Smith illustrated a new deck with Arthur

35
00:02:51,817 --> 00:02:57,343
Waite. For the first time, all 78 cards were given full scenes.

36
00:02:57,343 --> 00:03:01,313
It became the most used tarot in the world.

37
00:03:01,313 --> 00:03:06,206
And for decades, almost no one remembered her name.

38
00:03:07,399 --> 00:03:10,987
Today, tarot is art. A tool for reflection.

39
00:03:10,987 --> 00:03:13,168
A piece of pop culture.

40
00:03:13,168 --> 00:03:18,111
And in France and Italy, it's still played as a card game.

41
00:03:19,042 --> 00:03:23,239
So the next time you see a tarot card, remember:

42
00:03:23,239 --> 00:03:29,610
it all began with a game painted in gold, in Milan, nearly 600 years ago.

